#N Gosper glider gun
#C The first known finite pattern with unbounded growth, found by Bill Gosper
#C in 1970. It emits one glider every 30 generations, so the population grows
#C without bound while the gun itself returns to its starting shape.
#C
#C Placed near the centre of the 2^64 x 2^64 universe: the 36-wide gun is
#C centred on 2^63, so its top-left sits at 2^63-18, 2^63-4.
#C
#C The CXRLE line below is Golly's Extended RLE metadata, so the absolute
#C position is restored by other tools rather than merely tolerated as a
#C comment. Pos is signed, which costs nothing here on a 2^64 torus: both
#C readings of the same 64 bits name the same cell. This origin is below
#C 2^63, so its signed reading is unchanged. See patterns/glider.rle for the
#C case where it is not.
#CXRLE Pos=9223372036854775790,9223372036854775804
x = 36, y = 9, rule = B3/S23
24bo$22bobo$12b2o6b2o12b2o$11bo3bo4b2o12b2o$2o8bo5bo3b2o$2o8bo3bob2o4b
obo$10bo5bo7bo$11bo3bo$12b2o!
